This interpreter evaluates closures but captures the *variable object* instead of the *current value*, causing all closures in a loop to see the final value:

```python
def eval_lambda(node, env):
    # env is a mutable dict
    return ('closure', node.params, node.body, env)

def eval_call(closure, args, call_env):
    _, params, body, captured_env = closure
    new_env = dict(captured_env)  # shallow copy
    for p, a in zip(params, args):
        new_env[p] = eval_expr(a, call_env)
    return eval_expr(body, new_env)
```

When `env` is mutated after the lambda is created (e.g., in a loop), all closures share the same dict. Fix this so closures capture a snapshot of the environment at creation time.

